---
import CorpusLayout from '../../layouts/CorpusLayout.astro';
import { loadCorpusCatalog } from '../../lib/corpus/release';
import { recordingDateEstimate } from '../../lib/corpus/date-estimates';
import { describeDateBasis } from '../../lib/corpus/date-notes';

const catalog = await loadCorpusCatalog();
const fmt = (n: number) => n.toLocaleString('en-US');
const dated = catalog.recordings.filter((r) => r.date_label);
const undated = catalog.recordings.filter((r) => !r.date_label);
const byBasis = new Map<string, number>();
for (const r of dated) byBasis.set(describeDateBasis(r.date_basis), (byBasis.get(describeDateBasis(r.date_basis)) ?? 0) + 1);
const kinds = new Map<string, number>();
for (const r of undated) {
  const e = await recordingDateEstimate(r.recording_id);
  const label = e?.label ?? 'No date or estimate';
  kinds.set(label, (kinds.get(label) ?? 0) + 1);
}
const order = ['Date from matching text', 'Date from archived listing', 'Date from combined evidence', 'Date in title', 'Estimated date', 'Estimated period', 'No date or estimate'];
const estimateRows = [...kinds].sort((a, b) => order.indexOf(a[0]) - order.indexOf(b[0]));
---
<CorpusLayout title="How recordings are dated | HIMR Public Corpus" description="Where each recording's date comes from, how estimated dates are made and labelled, and how accurate each method has been when checked.">
  <nav class="corpus-breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/corpus/">Corpus</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span aria-current="page">How recordings are dated</span></nav>
  <header class="dates-header">
    <p class="corpus-eyebrow">Dates and estimates</p>
    <h1>How recordings are dated</h1>
    <p>Every date here is an upload or stream date. It says when a recording was published, not when the events it describes happened. {fmt(dated.length)} of {fmt(catalog.recordings.length)} recordings have a catalog date; the rest show a labelled estimate where one can be made.</p>
  </header>
  <article class="dates-copy">
    <section>
      <h2>Catalog dates</h2>
      <p>A catalog date is a date that the platform or the archive displayed for that very recording.</p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Where the date comes from</th><th scope="col">Recordings</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{[...byBasis].sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1]).map(([label, n]) => <tr><td>{label}</td><td>{fmt(n)}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
      </table>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Checked against independent evidence.</strong> 1,784 catalog dates were compared with archived copies of channel listings or video pages. All agreed to within a day.</li>
        <li><strong>One day of slack.</strong> Older archive files carry the day YouTube displayed (US Pacific time) and newer ones the UTC day, so the same video can appear a day apart in two sources.</li>
        <li><strong>Kick streams.</strong> Their archive file names use a clock that runs ten hours ahead of UTC. Catalog dates for those streams are the UTC start day, which is one day earlier than the file name for about half of them. Two independent checks support this: archived clip times, and the weekday and clock times said in the streams.</li>
        <li><strong>Re-uploads.</strong> A few recordings are later re-uploads of older material. Their date is right for the upload; the content is older.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Estimates for recordings without a catalog date</h2>
      <p>Estimates never replace a catalog date. Each one says what it rests on, on the recording's page and wherever the recording is listed.</p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Kind of estimate</th><th scope="col">Recordings</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{estimateRows.map(([label, n]) => <tr><td>{label}</td><td>{fmt(n)}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
      </table>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Matching text.</strong> The transcript is identical to, or shares most of its text with, a dated recording. Where an archived listing or video page confirms the date, lists show only the dated copy.</li>
        <li><strong>Archived listing.</strong> An archived copy of the channel's video list showed the video as "N days, weeks or months ago". YouTube rounds such ages down, which gives a window. All 505 videos with a known date fell inside their window when this was tested.</li>
        <li><strong>Combined evidence.</strong> A range of a week or less from several clues together: an archived stream record, a date label in an earlier transcript index, a weekday or date said in the recording, or the release date of something mentioned. Shown as "circa".</li>
        <li><strong>Date in title.</strong> The title itself contains a date.</li>
        <li><strong>Estimated period.</strong> An undated clip from an archived collection of Twitch clips is given the period that the collection's dated clips cover.</li>
        <li><strong>Estimated date (wording).</strong> A statistical estimate from the words used, given as a month with a range. On recordings with known dates it lands within three months at the median and within a year for 83%. Where release dates of things mentioned, spoken weekdays or similar clues narrow the range, the estimate says so. Tested on 3,039 dated recordings, the stated ranges contained the true date about 85% of the time.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Limits</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>A release date of a game or phone only gives an earliest date, and only when the recording shows it being used. Mentions alone are not used, because things are talked about before they exist.</li>
        <li>A weekday said aloud matches the catalog weekday about three times in four, and is within a day more than nine times in ten. Recordings are sometimes made a day before they are uploaded.</li>
        <li>Date labels from the earlier transcript index agreed with archived evidence in 11 of 12 cases that could be checked. They are plausible, not established.</li>
        <li>Recordings with no transcript, or a very short one, usually have no estimate.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </article>
</CorpusLayout>
<style>
  .dates-header{max-width:52rem;margin-bottom:1.5rem}.dates-header h1{margin:.2rem 0 .6rem}.dates-header p:last-child{color:var(--corpus-muted)}
  .dates-copy{display:grid;gap:2rem;max-width:60rem}.dates-copy>*{min-width:0}.dates-copy h2{margin:0 0 .6rem}
  table{width:100%;max-width:40rem;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.9rem;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;margin:.6rem 0}th,td{text-align:left;padding:.35rem .6rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--corpus-line)}th{color:var(--corpus-muted);font-weight:600}td:last-child,th:last-child{text-align:right}
  li{margin:.35rem 0}
</style>
